Music Assistant changes to libraop's src/rtsp_client.c, against the pinned
submodule commit. The build applies this to a copy of the file (see the
Makefile).

A failed connect closes its socket. Left open, the cleanup after it sends
FLUSH and TEARDOWN on a socket that never connected and waits 10 s for
each answer.

Only one request and its answer run on the connection at a time. libraop's
control thread sends its keepalive on the connection the caller's own thread
uses for volume, metadata, artwork and FLUSH. Left to overlap, each thread
reads parts of the other's answer, and one request can land in the middle of
another's body.

No request goes out on a connection the receiver closed. libraop only
recognises a connection that is down on both sides, so the cleanup's FLUSH and
TEARDOWN are otherwise sent and each log a failed request: after the receiver
ended the session itself (another sender took over), and on macOS after our
own TEARDOWN, where a connection the receiver closed first cannot be shut
down.

diff --git a/src/rtsp_client.c b/src/rtsp_client.c
index d71fa13..52832ca 100644
--- a/src/rtsp_client.c
+++ b/src/rtsp_client.c
@@ -48,16 +48,32 @@ typedef struct rtspcl_s {
 		char realm[16], nonce[256+1];
 		char ha1[32+1];
 	} digest;
+	pthread_mutex_t mutex;
 } rtspcl_t;
 
 extern log_level 	raop_loglevel;
 static log_level	*loglevel = &raop_loglevel;
 
-static bool exec_request(rtspcl_t *rtspcld, char *cmd, char *content_type,
+static bool exec_request_locked(rtspcl_t *rtspcld, char *cmd, char *content_type,
 			 char *content, int length, int get_response, key_data_t *hds,
 			 key_data_t *kd, char **resp_content, int *resp_len,
 			 char* url);
 
+/*----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+// several threads share the connection: one request and its answer at a time
+static bool exec_request(rtspcl_t *rtspcld, char *cmd, char *content_type,
+			 char *content, int length, int get_response, key_data_t *hds,
+			 key_data_t *kd, char **resp_content, int *resp_len,
+			 char* url) {
+	if (!rtspcld) return false;
+
+	pthread_mutex_lock(&rtspcld->mutex);
+	bool rc = exec_request_locked(rtspcld, cmd, content_type, content, length, get_response, hds, kd, resp_content, resp_len, url);
+	pthread_mutex_unlock(&rtspcld->mutex);
+
+	return rc;
+}
+
